{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And of your seed you shall not give to pass to Molekh.</b> This was the idolatry whose name was Molekh, and the rule of its worship was that the father would pass his son between two fires until he would burn and die, and their notion was that by the father's passing one of his sons, the rest of the sons would be saved and live, and he would succeed in all that he would undertake. And it mentions Molekh with the definite article, because it was known among them from Egypt, and it is called Molekh from the root of kingship and lordship, and it is the one called Milkom, which was the abomination of the children of Ammon, and it is the one called Adrammelekh and Anammelekh, as it is written (II Kings 17:31): ״והספרוים שורפים את בניהם לאדרמלך וענמלך אלהי ספרוים״. It is called עדר (adar) from the root of honor, and ענן (anan) from the root of 'to respond/afflict'; and each and every name is combined with the root מלך (melekh), which is the same root as מולך (molekh). If so, Molekh, Milkom, Adrammelekh, and Anammelekh — all are one idolatry called by all these names. <b>But the Gaon Rabbeinu Chananel</b> explained that Adrammelekh and Anammelekh are each a separate idolatry unto itself, and this is what is written in his commentary in the section of Ki Tissa: there are many kinds among idolaters — one makes his idol in the form of a man, and one makes the form of a cherub or the form of an animal or beast or bird, as it is written (ibid.): ״ואנשי בבל עשו את סכות בנות ואנשי כות עשו את נרגל ואנשי חמת עשו את אשימא, והעוים עשו את נבחז ואת תרתק והספרוים שורפים את בניהם באש לאדרמלך וענמלך״ — all of these are names of idols: Sukkoth-Benoth — the hen and her chicks; Nergal — the wild cock, which is the 'shakhvi'; Ashima — the cat; Nivkhaz — the dog; Tartak — the donkey; Adrammelekh — the monkey; Anammelekh — the peacock." "<b>And I am inclined</b> to say that the idols were named after the names of the stars, as it is written (Amos 5:26): ״ונשאתם את סכות מלככם ואת כיון צלמיכם כוכב אלהיכם אשר עשיתם לכם״ — סכות מלככם are names of stars, as it is written (Jeremiah 44:19): ״אנחנו מקטרים למלכת השמים״ — he called the stars 'the Queen of Heaven.' כיון צלמיכם is the one called זחל (zahal), which is Saturn; כוכב אלהיכם is also one of the seven wandering stars and is called 'the star of the Sun.' This far is the language of the Gaon, of blessed memory. And those idols would draw power from the stars; and it was called כיון because they would prepare and present before it various kinds of foods, for that was the manner of its worship; or because they would direct their mind and heart to them and prepare them with great intention, and this is what is written (Jeremiah 7:18): ״לעשות כונים״, and written (ibid. 44): ״עשינו לה כוונים״. As for the word נבחז — there are books in which it is written with a נו״ן. And the sage Rabbi Abraham says that it is none other than with a זיי״ן, and it is a compound word based on the fact that it barks (נובח) and bares its teeth." "<b>And that</b> which it mentions 'to pass to Molekh' and does not say 'to pass in the fire' — it is a well-known thing that the burning was in fire, for thus it is written (Deuteronomy 18:10): ״מעביר בנו ובתו באש״, and it is written of Ahaz the father of Hezekiah (II Kings 16:3): ״וגם את בנו העביר באש כתועבת הגוים״, and therefore the Rabbis, of blessed memory, needed to say of Hezekiah that his mother smeared him with salamandra. And it is written further (Ezekiel 23:37): ״וגם את בניהן אשר ילדו לי העבירו להם לאכלה״. But the view of the Rabbis, of blessed memory, regarding what it says, 'And of your seed you shall not give to pass to Molekh' — and does not say 'to pass in the fire' — is that this was in order to hold him liable from the moment of passing, even if the fire had only begun to take hold of him, such as when the fire caught one of his limbs. And that which Scripture juxtaposed this verse, which is a warning against idolatry, to the prohibition of a married woman — it may be explained in the matter of the juxtaposition that just so the Holy One, blessed be He, is jealous for Israel when they serve other gods, as a husband is jealous for his wife when she goes to others; and so we find in the matter of idolatry: ״כי אנכי ה' אלהיך אל קנא״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:26:48.497342495Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}